  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Isolated severe thunderstorms into the afternoon.\nDry tonight and Saturday.\n\nA broad trough of low pressure will traverse across the area\nthis afternoon, while a slow moving cold front bisects the area\nalong I-95. Isolated severe thunderstorms should develop into\nthe afternoon, mainly along and east of I-95 and across central\nVA. As of 10am, showers are popping up over northern portions of\nthe Chesapeake Bay with expectations of additional pop-up\nthunderstorms over the next couple of hours from northeastern MD\nsouth and west into the VA Piedmont and over into southern MD.\nOverall coverage and intensity should be significantly less \nthan what we had yesterday. Storms should quickly dissipate by \n00Z tonight, although they may linger a little longer across \ncentral VA, around Fredericksburg, and across St. Mary's County.\n\nDry conditions are anticipated Sat-Sat night as weak high\npressure builds in. Storm chances return Sunday afternoon as \nsoutherly winds return and weak surface troughing develops.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Hot and humid conditions next week with increasing \nthunderstorm chances by midweek.\n\nModels are generally in good agreement that a strong mid-level ridge \nbuilds across the southern U.S. and into the Mid-Atlantic during the \nearly to middle part of next week. Well above normal temperatures \nfor late August can be expected as highs reach the lower to mid 90s. \nHot and humid conditions are likely as heat indices reach the upper \n90s and possibly the lower 100s. \n\nA more notable mid-level disturbance will progress from the northern \nHigh Plains to the Northeast toward the middle of next week. The \nridge will be suppressed as a result, but it's uncertain by how much \nand how quickly. For now, Monday and Tuesday look mostly dry, though \ncannot rule out a stray thunderstorm or two given the humid/unstable \natmosphere. There is a greater chance the upper ridge begins to \nretreat slightly by Wednesday, allowing the shortwave troughs to \nmove over our area. Several of the AI models indicate increasing \nsevere potential at some point Wednesday to Friday of next week, \nwhich is certainly possible given the high instability that will be \nin place, along with substantial upper-level support from any strong \ndisturbance. It's also possible a back door cold front tries to drop \ninto the area by Wednesday or Thursday, leading to greater \nuncertainty in temperatures.",
